course: dictionary
lesson: 16064
title: A word from sewing: voile
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words name a kind of cloth. Voile is a thin, light cloth that you can see through, and people use it for curtains, dresses and blouses. «The bedroom curtains were voile, so the morning sun poured in.» The sun gets through the cloth, so you can guess it is see-through.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «Voile curtains let light in but stop people outside from seeing in easily.» Light comes in, but you cannot see clearly through the cloth. The clues show that voile lets light through while still giving some privacy.
explain: Try swapping in your meaning. «She sewed a blouse from light cotton voile.» If you put "thin, light, see-through cloth" in place of voile, the sentence still makes sense. That tells you your meaning is right.
fact: Voile means a thin, light, see-through cloth made of cotton, silk or polyester.
fact: Voile comes from French, where it means "veil".
fact: Voile is used for curtains, dresses and blouses.
fact: You may see the phrases «voile curtains» and «cotton voile».
fact: Voiles, with an s, means different kinds of voile cloth.
exercise: «The bedroom curtains were voile, so the morning sun poured in.» What is voile? | A heavy metal chain | A thick, warm blanket | *A thin, light, see-through cloth | A kind of wooden floor
exercise: «Voile curtains let light in but stop people outside from seeing in easily.» What do voile curtains do? | Stop the window from opening | Make the room very noisy | *Let light in but give some privacy | Block out all the light completely
exercise: Voile comes from a French word. Which language is it from? | Italian | German | Spanish | *French
test: «She sewed a summer dress from cotton voile.» Which materials can voile be made from? | Metal, wool or leather | Wood, paper or glass | Rubber or plastic sheets | *Cotton, silk or polyester
test: «Voile curtains let the light in but stop people seeing in easily.» Why are voile curtains used in homes? | To catch rain from the roof | *To let light in while giving some privacy | To keep the room dark and cold | To make the room loud
exam: «The shop sold voiles in many colours, for curtains and blouses.» What does voiles mean here? | Small cakes with cream | Pieces of old wood for building | Sails for a boat | *Different kinds of thin, light, see-through cloth
